---
import LanguageServerDoc from "@components/docs/LanguageServerDoc.astro";
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="YAML" description="YAML language-server support in Axon">
  <LanguageServerDoc
    title="YAML"
    description="Axon uses yaml-language-server for YAML configuration files. YAML appears in CI pipelines, Docker Compose files, Kubernetes manifests, deployment config, and tool settings, so schema-aware feedback is important."
    server="yaml-language-server"
    delivery="bundled npm server"
    activation="Starts for .yaml and .yml files"
    overview={[
      "YAML is flexible enough that syntax-only editing is not enough. The language server can validate indentation, structure, known fields, schema requirements, and common mistakes in configuration files.",
      "The server becomes much more useful when it can associate a file with a schema. A GitHub Actions workflow, Docker Compose file, or Kubernetes manifest can receive targeted completions instead of generic YAML suggestions.",
      "Axon should present YAML errors through the Problems panel and LSP logs so configuration mistakes are visible without opening developer tools.",
    ]}
    capabilities={[
      "Schema-aware completions for recognized YAML files.",
      "Diagnostics for indentation issues, invalid structure, schema mismatches, and malformed values.",
      "Hover details, document symbols, formatting, and validation for common YAML-based configuration formats.",
      "Better editing behavior for CI, Docker Compose, Kubernetes, and deployment files where schemas exist.",
    ]}
    setup={[
      {
        title: "Open the repository root",
        body: "Many YAML files are meaningful because of where they live. A workflow under .github/workflows, a compose file at the project root, or a Kubernetes manifest in deploy folders may need that workspace context.",
      },
      {
        title: "Schemas decide quality",
        body: "Without a schema, the server can still parse YAML, but completions and validation become less specific. Schema matching is what turns YAML support into practical project help.",
      },
      {
        title: "Treat packaging errors seriously",
        body: "The YAML server imports runtime modules from its npm package. Missing modules in release builds mean packaging is incomplete even if development mode works.",
      },
    ]}
    packaging={[
      "yaml-language-server is bundled as an npm server. Its dependency tree must be available to the packaged app.",
      "If production logs mention missing vscode-languageserver modules, the server package was not fully included or unpacked for runtime loading.",
    ]}
    troubleshooting={[
      {
        title: "YAML starts in dev but fails in release",
        body: "Check that yaml-language-server and the modules it requires are packaged outside the asar path where needed.",
      },
      {
        title: "No schema completions appear",
        body: "The file may not match a known schema. Check file name, folder location, and whether Axon has schema associations configured for that YAML type.",
      },
      {
        title: "Diagnostics are too generic",
        body: "Generic YAML parsing works without schemas, but tool-specific validation needs a matched schema.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
